Z||000007||LarryB||09-27-2001||11:05 AM||||FAKE!!!!! The image of the plane is way too sharp and the aspect is all wrong.<P>Even if the plane were moving at only 200MPH that translates to nearly 300 fps directly toward the camera and you'd get a lot of blur.  If they were flying at anywhere near their normal crusing speeds that would work out to be around 800-900 feet per second. <P>Don't bother speculating about ultra-high shutter speeds either, those theories can be debunked pretty quickly.  The way shutters work in normal cameras causes image distortion if they're used at hugely fast speeds, that's what strobe lights are for.<BR>And cameras with that capability don't put time stamps on the images, if they have that capability they place them in between the frames, so they don't show up on the actual pictures. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Voytek||09-27-2001||12:13 PM||||Other than that, the picture-taker would have seen the plane coming much earlier and probably ran like hell.  <P>They definitely wouldn't say 'Hey, could I get a photo of you and this plane that's coming right for us?'<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Voytek (edited 09-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cheever3000||09-27-2001||12:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>would have seen the plane coming much earlier<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>At 600 mph?  I don't think so.<BR>But there's no doubt it's a fake.  <BR>And I think whoever went to the trouble to make that is scum, even if they were just screwing around and not intending to pass it off as real.  It still takes a sick mind.<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||isthiswhereiputausername?||09-27-2001||01:51 PM||||they would have heard the roar and S**t themselves instead of taking a photo..<P>someone has a REALLY BAD sense of humor or is REALLY desperate to get their picture on tv..<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||09-27-2001||02:38 PM||||The biggest thing that shows it's faked, Look at the shadow on the guy. Look at the angle of the shadow. The sun is low in the sky. Now look at the plane, especially the left engine nacelle. This shows the angle of the sun as being high. If it was as low as the guys shadow, the body of the plane would cause a shadow along the wing at the same angle as the buildings in the background. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||09-27-2001||02:42 PM||||as far as being a fake - its not a bad job - the camera is facing north (thats the empire state building just to the guys left)<P>at 9am the sun is in the east, so the shadows on the guys face are correct<P>600mph =&gt;880 feet per second - so with a 1/1000th second shutter speed - normal in bright sunlite, the plane would have moved less than 1 foot during the exposure - you are not gonna see 1 foot of blur on an object 300 feet long moving towards you<P>the coat gives it away, the sky is too hazy, and IF a person was on the roof when the building was hit he would of had 30 minutes or more to try and get out, so the camera would be in the building when it collasped, not lying on top of the rubble.<P>Ill bet if the person that doctored this photo had thought of it, the national enquirer would have paid $50,000 for it. They doctor photos like this all the time, and publish them.<P> If nothing else, this proves you cant believe anything you see in a photo or video anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Ken Wittlief||09-27-2001||02:48 PM||||ok I just caught what you mean about high speed shutters - the shutter moves across the film as a narrow slit, and it takes about 1.100th of a second to make the full exposure - so the plane would have moved towards you by 8 feet, not 0.8 feet, during the exposure.<P> I still think you would have a hard time seeing that in something moving towards you - but I get your point - under a microscope you could detect it.<P> but I still wouldnt call it a 'blur'||||reg||1
Z||000014||maryjane||09-27-2001||02:56 PM||||real or not, the photo is disturbing. The mind that created is even more so. It's no big thing, but I don't like seeing sick scams like that created when we haven't even gotten most of the victims out yet. This is not directed at you, Fiero5, but rather at the scum that made it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fiero5||09-27-2001||08:04 PM||||The argument off the net goes like this:<P>The people might not be aware of the plane yet, as the photo taker would be squinting to see thru the small camera eye and consentrating on the subject. Either one of them might not hear the plane as the wind that high up the building could muffle it. Also, depending on the sound traveling as the plane came straight in, you might not hear it anyway until a second or so before it hit.<P>There is also the argument that the plane is coming in straight, and the actual plane was banking at impact. The first plane did come in straight as we saw from the video taken when the firefighter was being interviewed.<P>The only problem I have with the image is what the guy is wearing. Again, the argument is that it is breezy and a bit chilly that high up. I don't buy it. If I went to New York to sight see in early September (and it wasn't cool that day either), I don't think I would think of taking a thick coat and especially a wool cap.<P>Lastly, do we know if the 1st tower has a balcony? And is that the Boeing airplane and the correct airline?<P>Again, this is just my opinion.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>                                <IMG SRC="http://a1040.g.akamai.net/f/1040/759/1h/206.29.192.139/info/pics/sept11.jpg">                             <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||1
Z||000016||maryjane||09-27-2001||08:29 PM||||dunno, how cold is it that high up? Seems it wouldn't be that cold, I remember from my helicopter flying days and open doors.||||reg||1
Z||000017||DJRice||09-27-2001||09:30 PM||||I dont think either plane was cruising "straight and level" when they struck the tower either...||||reg||1
